## Why This Is Asked
This question tests practical amortized analysis under streaming appends with cache considerations. It evaluates understanding of how to design data structures that balance update frequency, query latency, and memory access patterns.

## Key Concepts
- **Sliding windows**: Maintaining a fixed-size view over streaming data
- **Circular buffers**: O(1) rotation without shifting elements
- **Amortized analysis**: Batched updates spread costs over time
- **Cache-friendly layout**: Sequential memory access patterns for spatial locality

## Code Example
```javascript
class StreamingAnalyticsStore {
  constructor(W, kQueries, mKeys) {
    this.W = W;
    this.blocks = [];
    this.head = 0;
    this.size = 0;
    this.windowSum = 0;
    this.queryRanges = kQueries.map(([start, end]) => ({ start, end }));
    this.keySet = new Set(mKeys);
  }

  appendBatch(newBlocks) {
    for (const block of newBlocks) {
      if (this.size === this.W) {
        this.windowSum -= this.blocks[this.head];
      }
      this.blocks[this.head] = block;
      this.windowSum += block;
      this.head = (this.head + 1) % this.W;
      this.size = Math.min(this.size + 1, this.W);
    }
  }

  rangeSum(queryIndex) {
    const { start, end } = this.queryRanges[queryIndex];
    const windowSize = this.size;
    const startIdx = (this.head - end + this.W) % this.W;
    const endIdx = (this.head - start - 1 + this.W) % this.W;
    let sum = 0;
    let i = startIdx;
    while (i !== (endIdx + 1) % this.W) {
      sum += this.blocks[i];
      i = (i + 1) % this.W;
    }
    return sum;
  }

  hasKey(key) {
    return this.keySet.has(key);
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests the ability to design efficient streaming algorithms that trade precision for memory and speed when computing quantiles—a fundamental requirement in real-time monitoring systems.

## Key Concepts
- Streaming quantile estimation using fixed-bin histograms
- Memory-accuracy trade-offs in one-pass algorithms
- Update complexity O(1) per element, query complexity O(K)
- Error analysis and parameter selection (K vs accuracy)
- Comparison with exact solutions using balanced search trees

## Code Example
```javascript
class FixedBinQuantile {
  constructor(M, K) {
    this.M = M;
    this.K = K;
    this.binWidth = Math.max(1, Math.ceil(M / K));
    this.bins = new Array(K).fill(0);
    this.total = 0;
  }

  add(x) {
    const idx = Math.min(this.K - 1, Math.floor(x / this.binWidth));
    this.bins[idx]++;
    this.total++;
  }

  getPercentile(p) {
    const target = Math.ceil(p * this.total);
    let cumulative = 0;
    for (let i = 0; i < this.K; i++) {
      cumulative += this.bins[i];
      if (cumulative >= target) {
        return (i + 1) * this.binWidth; // Upper bound of bin
      }
    }
    return this.M;
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests offline reasoning for dynamic bipartiteness under batched updates, along with the ability to combine DSU with rollback and time-block decomposition.

## Key Concepts
- **Parity (Bipartiteness) DSU**: Maintains connected components with parity labels to detect odd cycles, enabling O(α(N)) union-find operations.
- **Rollback / Persistent State**: Allows reverting DSU state within a block to test multiple edge configurations efficiently.
- **Time-based Block Decomposition**: Divides updates into blocks, rebuilding the DSU at block boundaries while processing updates within blocks using rollback.
- **Trade-offs**: Balancing rebuild cost (at block boundaries) against per-update cost (within blocks).

## Impact of Batch Size B
- **Larger B**: More edges processed per batch → fewer rebuilds → lower amortized cost per edge, but higher memory overhead for rollback structures.
- **Smaller B**: More frequent rebuilds → higher amortized cost, but lower memory since fewer rollback checkpoints are needed.
- **Optimal B**: Typically Θ(√M) balances O(√M · α(N)) per batch with O(M) space.

## Code Example
```cpp
struct ParityDSU {
    vector<int> parent, rank, parity;
    struct Change { int u, oldParent, oldRank, oldParity; };
    vector<Change> stack;

    ParityDSU(int n) : parent(n), rank(n, 0), parity(n, 0) {
        iota(parent.begin(), parent.end(), 0);
    }

    int find(int x) {
        if (parent[x] == x) return x;
        return find(parent[x]);
    }

    int findWithParity(int x) {
        if (parent[x] == x) return x;
        int p = findWithParity(parent[x]);
        parity[x] ^= parity[parent[x]];
        return parent[x] = p;
    }

    bool unite(int x, int y) {
        x = findWithParity(x);
        y = findWithParity(y);
        if (x == y) return parity[x] != parity[y];
        if (rank[x] < rank[y]) swap(x, y);
        stack.push_back({y, parent[y], rank[y], parity[y]});
        parent[y] = x;
        if (rank[x] == rank[y]) rank[x]++;
        parity[y] = parity[x] ^ parity[y] ^ 1;
        return true;
    }

    void snapshot() { stack.clear(); }
    void rollback() {
        while (!stack.empty()) {
            auto c = stack.back(); stack.pop_back();
            parent[c.u] = c.oldParent;
            rank[c.u] = c.oldRank;
            parity[c.u] = c.oldParity;
        }
    }
};
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design simple online statistics with fixed small memory. Mode is a practical KPI for signal quality in routing decisions on ride-hailing platforms. It requires maintaining counts and batch updates.

## Key Concepts

- Online counters on a small alphabet
- Tie-breaking and stability
- Time/memory trade-offs in streaming

## Code Example

```javascript
function OnlineMode() {
  const freq = [0,0,0,0,0,0]; // 1..5
  let mode = 1, modeCount = 0;
  return {
    update(batch){
      for (const r of batch){
        freq[r]++;
        if (freq[r] > modeCount || (freq[r] === modeCount && r < mode)){
          mode = r; modeCount = freq[r];
        }
      }
      return {mode, modeCount};
    }
  }
}
```

